{
  "_meta": {
    "purpose": "Map any abbreviation/name form found adjacent to a Stephanus/Bekker number to a canonical work id (matching ids in stephanus_ranges.json and bekker_ranges.json). Used by the abbreviation-match step: score adjacent tokens against these variants; let the number's range membership disambiguate ties.",
    "languages_covered": [
      "en",
      "la",
      "fr",
      "de",
      "it"
    ],
    "language_rationale": "Corpus roster is multilingual: Phronesis (en/fr/de/it/la), Revue de Philosophie Ancienne + Les Etudes philosophiques + Revue Internationale de Philosophie (fr), Methexis + Archivio di Filosofia (it), Classical Review/Quarterly/Philology (en, occasional la/de). So French/Italian/German/Latin abbreviations must all resolve.",
    "matching_guidance": [
      "Match case-insensitively and strip trailing periods.",
      "Match accent-insensitively for robustness (Rep./Rép./Repubblica) but PREFER an exact-accent match when scoring.",
      "Many forms are ambiguous ACROSS works (esp. 'Pol.', 'Resp.', book-letters). Ambiguous forms are listed under EVERY work they can denote, each with an 'ambiguous': true flag. The parser MUST NOT resolve an ambiguous abbreviation on the abbreviation alone - it defers to range membership + scope.",
      "The single worst collision is 'Pol.': Aristotle Politics (Bekker 1252+), Plato Statesman/Politicus (Stephanus 257-311), and older/Continental cites of Plato Republic. Format routing (Bekker vs Stephanus digit shape) resolves Aristotle-vs-Plato; range + scope resolves Statesman-vs-Republic on the Plato side.",
      "'Resp.'/'Rep.' collision: usually Plato Republic, but Aristotle De Respiratione is also 'Resp.' - format routing (Stephanus 327-621 vs Bekker 470-480) resolves it."
    ],
    "VERIFY": "Abbreviation lists are hand-built from standard conventions (OCD, LSJ, journal house styles) but are NOT exhaustive and may contain errors. Treat 'verified':false as 'sanity-check against real citations in the pilot data before trusting'. Continental forms especially need checking against actual French/Italian/German article usage once n-grams arrive."
  },
